digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56738-5
  rankdir="TB";
  "Imp_Concat" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: VGs can concatenate and multiple VG concatenate, therefore concatenation is a natural property."];
  "Imp_Dual_Mech" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Approach splits gene and inclusion of splice donor/acceptor allows reconstitution, therefore dual vector has specific mechanism."];
  "Imp_Enzyme" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Structure has one VG and no viral enzymes are packaged, therefore rAAV relies on external factors."];
  "Imp_Gap" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Specific pathways affect concatenation and HDR effect not examined, therefore HR role is the knowledge gap."];
  "Imp_ITR" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: VG requires ITR flanking and ITR directs packaging, therefore ITR is critical for VG."];
  "Imp_Insufficient" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Strategies employ cDNA/CMV and exclude larger genes, therefore they are insufficient for large genes."];
  "Imp_Limit" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: rAAV has limitations and capsid packages no more than 5kb, therefore payload is strictly limited."];
  "Imp_Pathway" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: MRN inhibits concatenation and NHEJ plays a role, therefore specific pathways affect concatenation."];
  "Imp_Safety" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: CMV leads to toxic levels and this leads to immune responses, therefore promoters cause safety issues."];
  "Imp_Significance" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: Findings emphasize host responses and results highlight pharmacological potential, therefore host modulation is significant."];
  "Imp_Unknown" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Reasoning: Steps orchestrated by host factors and steps remain observations, therefore host mechanism is unknown."];
  "Imp_Unverified" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: HR never directly tested and little known about AP, therefore AP/HR mechanism is unverified."];
  "Imp_Utility" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Reasoning: rAAV is a leading platform and has attractive features, therefore it is a valuable system."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates host DNA-repair responses to recombinant AAV vector genomes and proposes that early HDR/host homologous recombination activity can inhibit vector-genome concatenation, whereas blocking HDR to favor non-homologous end joining may improve split-transgene reconstitution, dual-vector rAAV gene-therapy efficiency, and effective payload capacity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Dual-vector effectiveness depends on correct directional and stoichiometric concatenation, and the current need for very high vector doses indicates that efficiency remains a practical constraint."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) AP fragments are used to increase the chance that left and right vector genomes join, and their proposed HR-mediated annealing mechanism remains a presumption rather than a demonstrated fact."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The AP-based dual-vector approach has an unresolved mechanistic basis because AP is presumed to act through HR while HR has not been directly tested as the concatenation mechanism."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Genome-wide identification of pathways inhibitory to split-transgene reconstitution, together with evidence for early chromatinization and DSB-like epigenetic recognition of vector genomes, implicates host DNA-damage responses in rAAV vector-genome processing."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Because host DNA-damage responses are implicated in rAAV vector-genome processing and blocking HR increases both vector-genome concatenation and expression, HR/HDR activity can act as an inhibitory response to rAAV vector genomes."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) If HR/HDR activity inhibits vector-genome concatenation while NHEJ is a more efficient concatenation mechanism, then shifting repair away from HDR and toward NHEJ is a plausible strategy to improve rAAV vector-genome concatenation."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) A host-cell-focused approach to improving dual-vector transduction can reasonably target host DNA-repair balance, including reducing HDR activity to favor more efficient NHEJ-mediated vector-genome concatenation."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) The study is motivated to develop and evaluate host DNA-repair modulation, especially HDR inhibition that favors NHEJ, as a way to enhance rAAV dual-vector concatenation and split-transgene reconstitution efficiency."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) The strict rAAV payload limit, together with the desirability of larger payloads for expressing large proteins, motivates approaches that improve effective payload capacity."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) After the capsid traffics the vector genome to the nucleus and is shed, the unclear conversion of ssDNA vector genomes to dsDNA makes early nuclear rAAV processing mechanistically unresolved."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Because rAAV lacks packaged viral enzymes and absence of viral enzymes implies host orchestration of circularization and concatenation, host cellular factors are expected to drive vector-genome concatenation."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) Since ITR-ITR joining is analogous to host DNA repair and host cells have HR/HDR and NHEJ pathways for double-strand break repair, DNA-repair pathways are plausible participants in vector-genome concatenation."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Dual-vector rAAV approaches exploit the propensity of vector genomes to concatenate by splitting a large transgene cassette into two halves that can be reconstituted after joining and splicing."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) Because effective dual-vector reconstitution requires correct directional and stoichiometric concatenation, random ITR joining that produces non-reconstituting permutations can reduce dual-vector efficiency."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) Given that HDR effects on vector-genome concatenation had not been directly examined, a host-cell-focused effort to improve dual-vector transduction should investigate host HDR activity in rAAV processing."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because rAAV is a valuable gene-delivery system but has a strict small-payload constraint, payload capacity is a central limitation for applying rAAV to larger therapeutic cargos."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Current rAAV strategies for large genes face limitations because some approaches exclude larger genes and CMV-based expression can create safety concerns, motivating improved delivery designs."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) rAAV are a leading platform for clinical DNA delivery."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) wt AAV and rAAV have same structure: capsid with one ssDNA VG."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) No viral enzymes are packaged within the virion."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) VG architecture requires flanking DNA with ITR sequences."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) ITR contains elements required for replication and directs packaging."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Capsid traffics VG to nucleus where capsid is shed."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) ssDNA VG is converted to dsDNA through unclear mechanisms."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Individual VGs can circularize and concatenate through ITR-ITR joining."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Multiple VG enter nucleus and can concatenate into large episomal species."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Absence of enzymes implies post-uncoating steps orchestrated by host cellular factors."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Nuclear steps remain observations with little mechanistic detail."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) rAAV has inherent features attractive for gene therapy."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Absence of viral enzymes implies circularization and concatenation orchestrated by host cellular factors."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) ITR-ITR joining is analogous to host DNA repair mechanisms."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Host cell has HR and NHEJ pathways to repair double-strand breaks."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) MRN complex recognizes ITRs but inhibits concatenation."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) NHEJ pathway has been suggested to play a role in concatenation."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Effect of HDR pathway on concatenation has not been directly examined."];
  "S26" [label="(26,0,0) Propensity of VGs to concatenate can be exploited to increase payload size."];
  "S27" [label="(27,0,0) In dual vector approach, large transgene cassette is split into two halves."];
  "S28" [label="(28,0,0) Inclusion of splice donor/acceptor allows ITR junction to be spliced out."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) rAAV has inherent limitations, major one is small genetic payload."];
  "S30" [label="(30,0,0) To be effective it requires correct, directional, and stoichiometric concatenation."];
  "S31" [label="(31,0,0) Any ITR can join any ITR, leading to permutations that do not lead to reconstitution."];
  "S32" [label="(32,0,0) The approach also currently relies on very high vector doses."];
  "S33" [label="(33,0,0) AP gene fragment can be added to increase chances of L and R genomes joining."];
  "S34" [label="(34,0,0) It is presumed that AP fragment promotes annealing of VGs through HR."];
  "S35" [label="(35,0,0) HR has never been directly tested as the mechanism of concatenation."];
  "S36" [label="(36,0,0) Little is known about the mechanisms underpinning the AP phenomenon."];
  "S37" [label="(37,0,0) Aim to improve dual vector transduction by an alternative approach that focuses on the host cell."];
  "S38" [label="(38,0,0) Cellular pathways inhibitory to split transgene reconstitution are identified by a genome-wide screen."];
  "S39" [label="(39,0,0) Evidence for early chromatinization of VGs and epigenetic recognition as a double-strand break is provided."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Capsid can package no more than 5kb of ssDNA."];
  "S40" [label="(40,0,0) Blocking HR increases both concatenation of and expression from rAAV VGs."];
  "S41" [label="(41,0,0) HR is suggested to be the first responder, but NHEJ is a more efficient mechanism for VG concatenation."];
  "S42" [label="(42,0,0) Findings emphasize the importance of understanding host responses in the target cell."];
  "S43" [label="(43,0,0) Results highlight the potential for pharmacological approaches to enhance efficiency."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Current strategies employ cDNA versions with CMV promoter."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Current strategy excludes larger genes such as dystrophin or Cas9."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) CMV promoters lead to off-target expression and toxic levels."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Off-target expression can lead to immune responses and therapy failures."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) A larger payload size is highly desirable for expressing large proteins."];
  "S1" -> "Imp_Utility" [label="induction-case"];
  "S2" -> "Imp_Utility" [label="induction-common"];
  "S3" -> "Imp_Limit" [label="deduction-case"];
